        {
            "number": 6,
            "revision": "c4dafe324b75dd9ea5b7f7b62b567bda941081ed",
            "parents": [
                "73b8ab4379b724d8ab6abc0d21d6e272d95833f2"
            ],
            "ref": "refs/changes/43/84643/6",
            "uploader": {
                "name": "Vijaysankar Ravi",
                "email": "vijaysankarr@ami.com",
                "username": "Vijaysankarr"
            },
            "createdOn": 1762496671,
            "author": {
                "name": "Vijaysankar Ravi",
                "email": "vijaysankarr@ami.com",
                "username": "Vijaysankarr"
            },
            "kind": "NO_CODE_CHANGE",
            "comments": [
                {
                    "file": "/PATCHSET_LEVEL",
                    "line": 0,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Vijaysankar Ravi",
                        "email": "vijaysankarr@ami.com",
                        "username": "Vijaysankarr"
                    },
                    "message": "Addressed Review comments"
                },
                {
                    "file": "user_mgr.hpp",
                    "line": 51,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Ravi Teja",
                        "email": "raviteja28031990@gmail.com",
                        "username": "raviteja-b"
                    },
                    "message": "why max PAM module count is 2 ?\n\nlooks like you considered only pam_unix & pam_ldap?\nthere are other pam modules like pam_google_authenticator.so integrated into openbmc to support multi factor authentication"
                },
                {
                    "file": "user_mgr.hpp",
                    "line": 51,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Vijaysankar Ravi",
                        "email": "vijaysankarr@ami.com",
                        "username": "Vijaysankarr"
                    },
                    "message": "The value 2 does not limit the number of PAM modules. It represents the current number of user categories supported by OpenBMC:\n\nInternal (local) users \u2192 authenticated by pam_unix.so\nExternal (LDAP) users \u2192 authenticated by pam_ldap.so\n\nAdditional PAM modules such as pam_google_authenticator.so, pam_faillock.so, etc., are secondary authentication steps and do not define separate user types. \nThey apply on top of the primary UNIX/LDAP authentication but do not authenticate users independently.\n\nIf OpenBMC introduces a third authentication source (e.g., RADIUS, Kerberos), this constant can be updated. \n\nFor now, OpenBMC only has two actual user types, so defined maxPamModuleCount = 2."
